    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Older conceptual geometric models suggest simple relationships between structural location on a fold, the local deformation (i.e. folding), the induced stress field and fracture orientation (Price 1966, Stearns 1969). These models do not take into account the complicated interactions among the remote tectonic stress, local folding or faulting induced stresses and pre-existing fractures that may influence the location and orientation of fractures that arise from the folding process. None-the-less, these older models, or modifications of them (Cooper et al. 2006), are used to explain the formation of fractures on folds (Wennberg et al. 2006). We suggest that such geometry-based models may be inadequate to explain the results of detailed studies of fracture characteristics on folds because they lack the kinematic constraints and mechanical insights necessary to understand the processes and sequence of fracture formation found on reservoir scale folds such as Raplee Ridge, UT.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
